## The library's arrays and algebra

- F-159 **In probes of 2026-09-29, a generic factory with a written static argument selected a store written for `RR64` alone, through a witness `typecase` whose branch result was passed through `cast`, on both paths.** A store is the object that holds an array's elements. A witness is `__thrower[\X\]`, a function of type `() -> X` that is never called; a `typecase` on it chooses a branch by the type `X`. The probes declared their own one-method trait `Arr[\T\]` and two stores, `DStore` for `RR64` and a generic `BStore`; they used none of the library's array traits, and the compiled runs used the compiler's own prelude. A witness `typecase` whose `RR64` branch returns `DStore` ran under walk. The compiled checker refused the body, whose type is the union `OR(DStore,BStore[\T\])`, against the declared result `Arr[\T\]`. With that branch's result passed through `cast[\Arr[\T\]\]`, as the library's `additiveIdentity` does, it checked. Both paths then gave `DStore` for `mk[\RR64\](r)`, with `r: RR64`, and `BStore` for `mk[\ZZ32\](z)`. A plain factory for `RR64` beside the generic one, called from generic code, was accepted by the compiled checker and dispatched to the plain declaration. Walk refused that pair at load; its present load check (F-27), which reads a generic declaration beside a plain one on their declared domains, has not been run on it. The library's `array1` and `array2` take a witness of their result type, not of the element type, for the reason F-229 gives. It bears on array decision A, how a `double[]` store goes under a float vector, which is open (P-85). No gated test holds it, and the probes are in the history before `84aef6a4f`. Detail: `reviews/array-design-ways.md` sections 0 and 3.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reworded (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f15/proposals.md, "F-159"); the claim bounded to its probes: their day and base (main at 6bc21c364, the note's section 1), their own trait Arr[\T\], the written static argument and the compiler's prelude on the compiled side (git show 84aef6a4f^:explorations/reviews/array-design-ways/probes/WayACast.fss); "store" and the witness __thrower said in words (the note's section 0); the cast placed on the branch's result; the plain factory's walk refusal restored from WayASpec.walk.txt, and F-27's later check (8f7e183d4) kept apart; "the library's factories" narrowed to array1 and array2 (FortressLibrary.fss:2247, :2284 still take an element witness); decision A said in P-85's words
- F-229 **`array1` and `array2` give a vector and a matrix only at the eight number types, chosen by a `typecase` over a witness of their result type; at any other element type they give a plain array.** The branches are `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `NN32`, `NN64`, `ZZ`, `QQ`, `RR32` and `RR64`; each builds the array at that type and returns it through `cast`, in `additiveIdentity`'s shape. `Array1` and `Array2` are invariant, so a branch matches only its own number type exactly. `Number` and `AnyIntegral` get a plain array, since `Vector[\Number,n\]` is not well-formed under the element bound `T extends { Number, MultiplicativeRing[\T\] }` of `Vector` and `Matrix` (F-151). The specification's "Array Expressions" makes an array of `Number` elements a vector (row 729, contested). Under walk, a type parameter inferred from a `ZZ32` value is `Int`, the one type that `ZZ32` comprises (row 364), and one inferred from a `ZZ64` or `RR64` value is that value's run-time class. Such a type parameter gets a plain array, where the specification gives a vector (row 721). The witness is of the result type because at `T = Int` a witness of the element type, `() -> Int`, would match the `ZZ32` branch, and the `cast` of the `ZZ32` vector to an array of `Int` would throw `CastError`, as `openRange` does (row 722). Gated under walk by `tests/ArrayFactoryNumberTypes` and `ArrayFactoryAbstractNumber`. The distance stage measures the compiled checker's acceptance; it reports, and does not turn the gate red. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-library-factories/REPORT.md` sections 1 (the change), 3 and 4 (the ways measured).
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f15/proposals.md, "F-229"); the long sentence split into its three claims, each with its pointer; walk's Int said as ZZ32's one listed type (FortressLibrary.fsi:532-534; row 364), and row 721 put back on walk's plain array; the inverted clause on the element witness corrected (the report's section 4; row 722); "gated by the distance stage" corrected, since the stage reports only (coordinator skill, references/batch.md:50); moved here: "The four wellformed distance sites of the old factories are gone (row 685)", repair history, and the lines Library/FortressLibrary.fss:2572-2586 and :2953-2965, now :2585 and :2972 (the report's section 1 holds them at its commit)

## The specification and the repository's lineage

- F-162 **Citing `Specification/library/apis/*.tex` as an independent standard is circular.** The team says that the library part "is largely automatically generated from the API code" (`Specification/library/structure.tex:25-31`). The build renders these listings from the `.fsi` files of `Library/` and `ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/`, and the listings are not in the tree. So a listing cannot justify an edit of an `.fsi` file: the edit changes the listing in the next built PDF. Cite the `.fsi` line. The `fortress-repo` skill's `references/specification.md` holds the rule.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reworded (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f15/proposals.md, "F-162"); the two unclear referents, "they" and "there", made plain; the rendered folders from Specification/fortress/build.xml:128-156
- F-163 **Of the two copies of the specification, `Specification/` is the one to search and change; `Specification-1.0-frozen/` is byte for byte the Working Draft of 2011-02-02, and only its `fortress.1.0.pdf` is the 1.0 text.** `git diff --quiet 2f8b4331a:Specification c02fa44d0:Specification-1.0-frozen` succeeds; `2f8b4331a` is the team's last commit of the draft (2011-02-02). In the frozen copy, Appendix I, the changes appendix, is a stub that the team left; in `Specification/` it holds the revival's changes (P-96). `Specification/fortress.pdf` is a draft build, so the team's `\note` boxes print. A third text, the team's unfinished restart, is later on some topics (F-166). The `fortress-repo` skill's `references/specification.md` says how to cite and change each copy. Detail: `reviews/spec-change-form.md` sections 5 and 7.
