- F-168 **The specification's number chapters describe the flat library, and its advanced chapter on rational quantities stays word for word under a callout that marks it superseded.** The eight number types are siblings under `Number` (F-131). They convert by the library's coercions (P-74), and every other conversion is explicit, such as `asFloat(x)`. The sign-refined types, such as positive ℚ, are gone; a program tests a value's sign at run time (P-80). ℚ holds +∞, −∞ and 0/0, and claims neither a field nor a total order. `QQ` extends the four algebraic traits that the library declares on it, `StandardPartialOrder`, `StandardMinMax`, `AdditiveGroup` and `MultiplicativeRing`, and their laws hold for the finite rationals. F-172 gives the integer chapter's rules. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-spec-numbers/decision-record.md` section 3; `Specification/basic-lib/numbers.tex:224-229` (ℚ's traits); `Specification/advanced-lib/numbers-advanced.tex:14` (the callout).
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f15/proposals.md, "F-168"); "The integer ^ has no result for a negative power", true when the rung landed (the record's section 5, decision 4) and false since 5ad4a9957 (basic-integers.tex:534-537), moved to F-172 corrected; "The chapters state P-78" dropped, since no number chapter names SUM or PROD and the reductions callout (basic/expressions/reductions.tex:27) is outside them; the refined types named (P-80); ℚ's traits moved here from F-172 and named (Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:395-396); the superseded chapter's callout cited
- F-169 **The Working Draft never wrote static-argument inference, never named the type that a library gives an integer numeral, and never named a range's integer type; the specification's text on these is the revival's.** The Working Draft's inference chapter is a heading and notes. It described numeral types, such as `NaturalNumeral[\n,10,v\]`, and said that libraries define coercions from numerals to integers and rationals. But a note of its own leaves the numeral types' hierarchy undescribed, and it names no type that a library declares for a numeral. Its ranges section names no integer type. The later restart adds nothing on the three. So a citation of the specification on them cites the revival, not the team. The revised text states F-173's inference rule and F-170's ranges, and gives an integer numeral the type `IntLiteral` (P-76). Detail: `reviews/numerics-plan-coordinator/evidence-B.md` sections 1 and 2; `Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/expressions/literals.tex:87-96` and `:132-148` (the note and the coercions); `Specification/basic/expressions/literals.tex:154-156` (the callout on the numeral types).
  by: the clarity pass; note: reworded (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f15/proposals.md, "F-169"); "never wrote a numeral's type" narrowed: the Working Draft described numeral types and the libraries' coercions from numerals (evidence-B section 1.1; frozen literals.tex:83-148) but left their hierarchy undescribed and never named the type that a library gives a numeral (literals.tex:154-156), as P-76 also says
- F-170 **The specification's ranges are over ℤ32, and its own ranges over wider integer types are respelled.** An integer numeral, and a `nat` or `int` parameter, used as an operand of an explicit range, such as `a` or `n` in `a#n`, converts to ℤ32 (row 485, fixed). An operand of any other integer type is a static error. A loop whose counter must have a wider integer type runs over a range of ℤ32 values and widens each element. The two examples that built `ZZ64` ranges run over ℤ32, and `ant testSpecData`, a gate step, runs both (F-114). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-spec-ranges/decision-record.md` sections 2 and 3; `Specification/basic/expressions/ranges.tex:42-58` and `:88-109`.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f15/proposals.md, "F-170"); row 485 moved to the sentence it supports; "a range's ℤ32 components" said as the operands of an explicit range; the counter sentence written out (ranges.tex:88-89); moved here: "Under walk a small nat size used as a value, such as n with nat n = 3, is a signed ZZ32, which an NN32 binding refuses (row 486); F-140 gives walk's reading of a size used as a value", which F-140 and row 486 hold
- F-171 **The specification reads a `comprises` clause as the restart's Types chapter does: every value of the trait is a value of a listed type.** So a trait or object that extends the closed trait must be below a listed type, with two exceptions. A trait with a `comprises` clause of its own may stay unlisted if each of its own listed types is below a listed type of the closed trait. A trait with static parameters may stay unlisted if at least one type extends it and every type that extends it is below a listed type. A listed type may be an instantiation at the trait's static parameters or `where`-clause variables. The text does not say whether a bare type variable may be listed; walk overflows its stack on one (row 407). The compiled checker departs in three ways: it checks only the closed trait's immediate subtypes (row 487), it reads the clause in the subtrait's own type-variable names (row 489), and it finds extenders by the simple text of a name (row 490). Whether the team's commented `comprises Integral[\I\] where [\I\]` is valid is open (F-121). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-spec-comprises/decision-record.md` section 2; `Specification/basic/traits.tex:235-258`.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reworded (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f15/proposals.md, "F-171"); "may stand unlisted between" and "stays open" said in words, with both of the text's exceptions (Specification/basic/traits.tex:247-255; the record's section 2); rows 487, 489 and 490 given their departures
- F-172 **The specification's integer chapter states the library's integer rules.** `narrow` converts a `ZZ64` or `ZZ` to `ZZ32`, and an `NN64` to `NN32`, by keeping the low 32 bits; a value that does not fit wraps, and nothing is thrown. `LSHIFT` and `RSHIFT` work on the five integer types. On `ZZ` they are `shift`, which is exact at any count. On a fixed-size type, `LSHIFT` loses the bits shifted out and throws nothing. `GCD` and `LCM` are nonnegative, and one that a fixed-size type cannot hold throws `IntegerOverflow`. At a negative power, the bases 1 and -1 give their exact power, a base of 0 throws `DivisionByZero`, and any other base throws `IntegerOverflow`. The library follows the same power rule under walk; the compiled path's own library does not (F-263). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-spec-integer-rules/decision-record.md` section 2; `Specification/basic-lib/basic-integers.tex:39-62` (`narrow` and the shifts) and `:530-542` (the power); `Specification/basic/operators/opr-overview.tex:262-268` (`GCD` and `LCM`).
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f15/proposals.md, "F-172"); split: the coercion example is now F-?2, and ℚ's traits moved to F-168; the negative power added from F-168, corrected to the text since 5ad4a9957 (basic-integers.tex:534-537), with F-263's pointer; "a fixed-size result that the type cannot hold throws IntegerOverflow" bounded to GCD and LCM, its rule in the record's table, since narrow and LSHIFT throw nothing (basic-integers.tex:39-62)
- F-?2 **The coercion example of the specification's "Coercion Resolution", over its own traits `ZZ32`, `ZZ64` and `ZZ128`, declares the exclusions that the chapter's definitions need: `ZZ32 excludes { ZZ64, ZZ128 }` and `ZZ64 excludes { ZZ128 }`.** A type `T` rejects `U` when every type that coerces to `T` excludes `U`. `T` is no less specific than `U` when it is a subtype of `U`, or excludes, coerces to and rejects it. So the choice of the more specific declaration depends on the exclusions. In the rung's probe, with the traits renamed `S32`, `S64` and `S128`, since the first two names are the library's, the revised example ran on both paths: `f(S32Of)`, on an `S32` value, chose `f` on `S64` and gave 1. No gated test runs the printed example. Row 394, fixed, names `tests/CoercionMostSpecificRungC.fss`, a walk test of the choice of the more specific coercion over three traits of the example's shape, each of which excludes both others. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-spec-integer-rules/decision-record.md` section 4, decision 6; `Specification/basic/conversions-coercions.tex:500-516` (the definitions) and `:580` onward (the example).
